// Bit standard library — sql module: `.tx()`/`.txAt()`/`.txValue()`/
// `.txValueAt()` as METHODS on `Pool`, not free functions (#5055, owner
// decision: "I don't like that we have to pass the pool like this").
//
// ./tx.bit's `tx`/`txAt`/`txValue`/`txValueAt` stay the free functions they
// already were — this file adds no behaviour, only a call shape. Each
// method here is one line delegating to its free-function namesake, so
// `db.tx(f)` and `tx(db, f)` run the exact same code.
//
// A TRAIT, NOT METHODS WRITTEN ON `Pool` DIRECTLY (SPEC §10.7): a trait's
// provided methods are injected once and reused everywhere they are `use`d,
// so a second class that ever needs the same methods gets them with one
// `use Transactable` line instead of another copy. Today only `Pool`
// (./pool.bit) uses it — the transaction handle already gets this same call
// shape for free, because `tx`'s own `enter()` recognises a `scopedTx`
// passed back in and opens a SAVEPOINT instead of a new transaction; calling
// the free function on it directly (`tx(t, f)`) is a SEPARATE, unchanged
// path and not this ticket's concern.
//
// `txValue`/`txValueAt` are NOT here. `trait_method` is documented with an
// optional `generic_params` (SPEC §10.7's grammar), but the parser does not
// accept one on a trait member — confirmed here: `identity<T>(v: T): T`
// inside a bare `trait` fails `E0021 expected '(', found '<'`, both
// required and provided, where the identical line inside a `class` body
// parses and runs. `Pool.txValue`/`Pool.txValueAt` (./pool.bit) are declared
// directly on `Pool` instead — the one caller this ticket has — and filed
// as a compiler gap for whichever ticket next needs a second class with a
// generic transaction-returning method.
//
// `tx`/`txAt` are `export`ed (#5740): `Pool` is used from every package that
// wraps it (`pkg/mysql`, `pkg/orm`, `pkg/postgres` all call `pool.tx(...)`/
// `pool.txAt(...)` across the module boundary, documented public API —
// pkg/orm/docs/data.md, pkg/orm/README.md), so the injected copy must carry
// the same visibility `Pool`'s own `txValue`/`txValueAt` (./pool.bit) already
// declare directly with `export`. A trait is still never a type (SPEC §10.7)
// — this module's own `Executor` interface remains the type a caller wanting
// "a Pool or a live transaction" reaches for; `pkg/orm`'s `Data`
// (pkg/orm/data.bit) is that same shape under the ORM's own name.
trait Transactable {
  // Required of the implementor — `Pool` already declares both.
  query(sqlText: string, params: []Value): Rows!
  exec(sqlText: string, params: []Value): int!

  // Provided: one line each, delegating to ./tx.bit's free functions.
  export tx(f: (Tx) => ()!): ()! {
    tx(this, f)?
  }

  export txAt(level: Isolation, f: (Tx) => ()!): ()! {
    txAt(this, level, f)?
  }
}
